            <ArticleTitle>Endosomal Trafficking: Retromer and Retriever Are Relatives in Recycling.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>R1233-R1236</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0960-9822(17)31307-6</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.cub.2017.10.004</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Transmembrane proteins are sorted from endosomes to avoid lysosomal degradation. A recent study has identified a new multimeric complex called retriever that is essential for recycling numerous cell-surface cargoes from endosomes and is structurally and functionally related to the well-characterised retromer complex.</AbstractText>
                <CopyrightInformation>Published by Elsevier Ltd.</CopyrightInformation>
            </Abstract>
